which is the only way you learn

270
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
is: you can't learn from success

271
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:44,000
because you don't know what worked right.

272
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
So I would pick

273
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
a really small number of things,

274
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
like one or two, maybe three,

275
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
expecting that they're not all going to change.

276
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
if you want to get, say,

277
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:56,000
outings on a regular basis

278
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,000
and patrol camping on outings,

279
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:00,000
Just a couple of things.

280
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:03,000
It may take a long time for those to happen.

281
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:06,000
And then troop leadership training:

282
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:10,000
sit down with SPL patrol leaders,

283
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
have them set goals,

284
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
see if the SPL has things they want to do.

285
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
Maybe they have a better idea.

286
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
Mentor a little bit in the direction

287
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:22,000
of some things. you see that would help.

288
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
And when you're in doubt,

289
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
let the program roll.

290
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:31,000
What was your position prior to last night

291
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,000
and was this amicable?

292
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
Were you the first choice

293
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:39,000
or how did you become the scout master last night?

294
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
I've been involved with this troop since 2002..

295
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
There have been rocky times in the past,

296
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:46,000
but I would say over the last five, six years

297
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:49,000
the ASMs and the boys

298
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:52,000
and the committee have been working very well together

299
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:55,000
And I think for the most part

300
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:58,000
we adults have been fairly effective

301
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
in not trampling on the boys.

302
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:06,000
I was committee chair until 2009,

303
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:09,000
and since then I've been an assistant scout master.

304
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
I've sat in on a good number

305
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
of the patrol leader council meetings

306
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:18,000
So I have a fairly good rapport

307
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
with the boys and the current leaders

308
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,000
and I kind of understand what their style is.

309
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:27,000
And again, I already have gotten a lot of practice

310
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
trying to keep my hands in my pockets

311
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
and keep my mouth shut

312
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
so the boys can do their work.

313
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
But I'm not coming at this completely cold

314
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:39,000
and I have a relationship with the boys

315
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
and the leaders already, luckily.

316
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
You asked about the transition.

317
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
The transition was completely amicable.

318
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:48,000
Our previous scout master was doing a good job.

319
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:52,000
His youngest son is starting as a Tiger Cub

320
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
so he needs to kind of refocus his efforts

321
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
and he'd finished a three-year tenure.

322
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:59,000
We went through the standard process

323
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
looking for a new scout master

324
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
and I put my name in.

325
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:06,000
I don't know what order I was.

326
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
If you follow the process

327
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:11,000
as it's written in the BSA rules, you don't know.

328
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:14,000
And to be honest, it wouldn't matter

329
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:16,000
because I'm the scout master now,

330
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
regardless of how many candidates they went through.

331
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,000
Oh, you're stuck, brother.

332
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
Yeah,

333
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
One of the things that I've seen.

334
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
I'll get a lot of boys tell me

335
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:29,000
I did the square knot for this rank,

336
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:32,000
or I learned the clothe hitch for this rank

337
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
so I don't need to do that anymore.

338
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
Well, I know what I'd like to have happen,

339
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:39,000
but I've been concerned for a long time

340
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
that the attitude is: I did that requirement,

341
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
I'm done, I never have to worry about it again.

342
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,000
I was just curious about your comments on that.

343
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:50,000
Okay, That's a pep one for Larry.

344
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
Watch out.

345
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,000
If they're going to be Star Life or Eagle,

346
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:57,000
they're going to take a position of responsibility

347
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
and you guys have heard this before.

348
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
It's their job to lead, train and inspire

349
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:04,000
those younger scouts to achieve first class

350
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:07,000
and part of that job is teaching them those skills.

351
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
You know there's different ways to do that:

352
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:13,000
within the patrols or with instructor positions.

353
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
I never was good at backing off of that.

354
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:18,000
I kept telling them face to face. on some occasion

355
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:21,000
some of the older scouts- that's your job, you know,

356
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
And I mean the senior patrol leader-

357
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
would pick out two guys and say:

358
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:28,000
you know, you 16-year-old scout,

359
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
you go, take those two 11-year-olds,

360
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
learn Bolans or First Aid.

361
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:35,000
I mean you have to make it part of the culture of your troop

362
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
that that's what they do.

363
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
Everybody who's first class. it's their job

364
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:42,000
to be doing that stuff and it becomes a habit,

365
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
It becomes part of what your troop does.

366
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:48,000
As they teach the skills, they become real to them,

367
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
not just, oh, I did that,

368
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:54,000
It starts to become theirs to some extent

369
00:15:55,000 --> 00:16:02,000
Life has. you must do a list of requirements

370
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
using the EDGE method,

371
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:09,000
which is teaching others to do these skills,

372
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:12,000
And we've kind of come around.

373
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:16,000
I understand how many times can you tie a square knot?

374
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:19,000
But if you make a little game out of it,

375
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:23,000
like take a tent and strip all the ropes off it

376
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
and then have them pitch the tent,

377
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:29,000
well, lookie here, this is where this knot goes,

378
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:32,000
and oh, this line's not long enough to reach this tree.

379
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:35,000
so now we need to use a square knot to tie a longer line

380
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:42,000
and make it more real-time or real-life uses

381
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:47,000
for each of those knots and the advancements.

382
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:50,000
Well, we're lucky that we do have that culture

383
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:53,000
where the older scouts teach.

384
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
We do a lot of that scout skills instruction

385
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
in patrol meetings.

386
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
So we have a patrol meeting

387
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:04,000
Once. one night a month is the patrol meeting night,

388
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:08,000
And so the patrol leader is the one whose job it is

389
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
to get those other scouts to teach.

390
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
In troop leadership training.

391
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:16,000
I taught EDGE to the patrol leaders.

392
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
Some of them have set goals for

393
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
we want this many guys to advance,

394
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:23,000
Well, they need other guys to teach them

395
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
to get through that advancement.

396
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
My question, Maury,

397
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:31,000
is what percentage of instruction are scouts doing

398
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
And on the other side,

399
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:36,000
what percentage are adults doing? Clark,

400
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
In the last few years

401
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
they've been working very hard on having the boys

402
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
do all the training.

403
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
One thing that seems to have happened

404
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:47,000
is that we have troop guides and instructors

405
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:50,000
and so they become the ones that do the training

406
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
and meet with the younger boys,

407
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:55,000
but the other older boys just kind of feel like

408
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
they've got to pass.

409
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:03,000
Maybe our problem is not building the general culture

410
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:06,000
for boys first class and up.

411
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
But are adults instructing

412
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
For the most part? no, not anymore.

413
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
Okay, All right.

414
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
And who's signing off requirements?

415
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:17,000
ASMs can sign off requirements.

416
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
For the most part, our troop guides

417
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,000
are signing off on requirements up to first class.

418
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:25,000
You said you had fairly well-distributed patrols.

419
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:29,000
You could work on the culture that Walter's talking about

420
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
within that kind of a thing.

421
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
In my troops I've always had any scout

422
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:36,000
whose first class can work with you

423
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:39,000
on any requirement up to first class.

424
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:43,000
Any scout that's first class and above

425
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:47,000
should be doing some teaching

426
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
as part of their car and life ranks.

427
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
Okay, that's a good idea.

428
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
I have one question for Walter to follow up on.

429
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:56,000
Did Walter say that one troop night a month

430
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
is a patrol night?

431
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
That's right. One of the weekly meetings

432
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
is a patrol meeting and the patrols decide

433
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
where that is: the house, the rec room,

434
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
the apartment building or whatever.

435
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:10,000
The troop meeting plan has a time for patrol meetings.

436
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
I think the monthly patrol meeting

437
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
really forces the patrol leader.

438
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
There's not a bunch of scoutmasters standing around it.

439
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
I like that thing, Walter.

440
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:21,000
It really pushes them into their position.

441
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
It gives them a great opportunity

442
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,000
to actually do what they're up to.

443
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
Are you saying one of the nights

444
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
that would be a troop night

445
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
is instead when the patrols get together?

446
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
or you're just saying you make sure

447
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
patrols meet once a month at least?

448
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
No, it's regular troop meeting night.

449
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
You can already call your patrol leader

450
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
to find out where it is.

451
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
At the PLC meeting,

452
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:47,000
the SPL talks with the patrol leaders

453
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
about what they're going to do that night.

454
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
At the next meeting they talk about

455
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
what they did last time.

456
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:56,000
We do a similar thing.

457
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
All the patrols meet at the same place.

458
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
They meet at the church

459
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
where we have our regular troop meetings.

460
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
They do that at least once a month

461
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
and sometimes twice,

462
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
Suspending the troop meeting night,

463
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
putting it in the hands of the patrol leaders.

464
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
it's been good for everybody.

465
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
We have had patrol leaders

466
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
who decided to just go for the

467
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
National Honor Patrol

468
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
Because that actually has a nice.

469
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
that means you have two meetings a month.

470
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
They'll get the patrol together

471
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
at the end of the scout meeting

472
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
or sometimes say: okay, this is a meeting

473
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
and we're talking about what we're cooking

474
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
on the camp out.

475
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
You have to have people advance

476
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
coverage of what a patrol should be doing.

477
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
A good number of years ago

478
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
we seemed to have a lot more variety

479
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
in our troop meetings.

480
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
The boys seemed to have gotten stuck in a rut

481
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
and had very few ideas,

482
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
Very few original ideas, about

483
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
what they should be doing in troop meetings.

484
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
I suspect part of it is they're

485
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
really not working the program much anymore.

486
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
I wanted to get some ideas about

487
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
the universe of activities

488
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
that they might want to consider

489
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
for working in a troop meeting.

490
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
What I find is generally

491
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
our troop meeting plans

492
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
are driven by the outing

493
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
and whatever we're getting

494
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
ready to do that month

495
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
they're prepping for during troop meetings.

496
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
whatever skills

497
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
or other preparations

498
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
need to be made are all centered on

499
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
what's happening that month

500
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
where we're going camping

501
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
and what they have planned to do.

502
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
What's it like in your troop, Tom?

503
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
We're doing the same thing.

504
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
we've got the district campery

505
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
coming up and we know ahead of time

506
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
what the theme is.

507
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
There's a world of things right there.

508
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
but have you seen

509
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
the Wood Wisdoms

510
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
book?

511
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
Our senior patrol leaders have it.

512
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
I've never been able to get them to actually read it

513
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
or use it. It's got a lot of good ideas.

514
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
some of them are hard to implement,

515
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
but that's

516
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
a good place to start too.

517
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
The troop program helps.

518
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
the three-year plan is

519
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
one place. have them

520
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
pick a theme and then do the meetings

521
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
At some point. our guys started

522
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
doing monthly themes,

523
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
so they'll at the beginning,

524
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
at the annual planning meeting,

525
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
they'll decide on monthly things. Some of them are

526
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
things like:

527
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
we have the new guys showing up

528
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
in March and so

529
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
we need to do a thing about backpacking

530
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
and stuff like that so they'll be ready to go in outings.

531
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
Some of them we've got to do

532
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
first aid at some point, so there's a lot of stuff that gets repeated.

533
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
We do sing on winter

534
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
camping and clothing and hypothermia

535
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
every year. The monthly themes

536
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
do help. Again,

537
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
I've not been able to get anybody that interested

538
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
in the troop program features.

539
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
I have occasionally

540
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
got them to use the games.

541
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
it's a great list of games.

